      "knowledge_point": "Differentiating a Polynomial Raised to a Power Using the Chain Rule",
      "problem": "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(4+6x{)}^{3}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{3},\\,g=4+6x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=3{g}^{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=6.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =3\\cdot 6\\cdot (4+6x{)}^{2} \\\\  & =18(4+6x{)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
