      "knowledge_point": "Differentiating a Function Using the Chain Rule and Prime Notation",
      "problem": "Given that $f(x)={(3x+4)}^{5},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $g(x)=3x+4$ and $f(g(x))=g(x{)}^{5}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{5}{)}^{′}=5{g}^{4}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(3x+4{)}^{′}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =5{g}^{4}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =15(3x+4{)}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
